<!--
  AP session-tx buffer-pool config: the SSOT for the slots a tokio (AP / Linux)
  stream link's outbound frames live in, from the moment the session is lent
  one until the link's writer task has written it (ARCHITECTURE section 9.1:
  "Codec::encode writes directly into a pool slot").

  This is the migration R2739 made for the receive pool, made for the transmit
  one, and it is born with its consumer: `wz-runtime-tokio`'s writer queue,
  behind `runtime-zero-copy`. The AP node declared this pool inline at
  `deploy/ap_mcu_pair.yaml` `ap_node.buffer_pools.session_tx_pool` with no
  scxml SSOT and no emit, so nothing consumed it and nothing graded its
  values; the deploy entry is now `{ ref: session_tx_pool_ap }`.

  ## One pool per LINK, not per node

  The receive pool is the node's because a link's read half holds at most one
  frame at a time. A writer queue holds as many frames as its lanes admit, and
  a link whose peer stops reading keeps them; a node-wide table would let one
  such link take every slot from every other link. Upstream sizes its transmit
  batches per link for the same reason (each link's transmission pipeline owns
  `queue_size` batches per priority, of the link's batch size). So each
  stream link's queue owns one instance of this pool.

  ## slot-size 65600 is DERIVED from the largest frame, not chosen

  A stream frame is the length prefix and the payload: the prefix is 2 bytes
  on the universal path and 4 under `transport-lowlatency`, and the payload is
  at most `u16::MAX` because the prefix cannot say more and the write half
  drops a larger frame. So the largest frame is 4 + 65535 = 65539 bytes, and
  any frame the session can hand a stream link fits ONE slot: a frame past the
  old 16 KiB spare bound is not a special case. Rounded up to the 64-byte
  alignment below, as `session_rx_pool_ap.scxml` derives the same figure on the
  receive side: 65600.

  A slot holds frames back to back, written by one write: the writer queue
  appends a frame to its lane's newest slot while that slot has room and the
  writer has not taken it, which is what upstream's batches do on the same
  stream. On a byte stream the concatenation is the wire, byte for byte.

  ## slot-count 16 is DERIVED from the queue's own bound

  The writer queue admits frames into a lane while the lane holds less than
  `queue_size * batch_bytes` bytes (zenoh's default queue size is 2 for every
  priority, and its batch is at most 65535 bytes). Frames pack into slots, so a
  slot that is not the lane's newest is more than half full unless a frame of
  more than half a slot did not fit behind it; a lane at its bound therefore
  holds at most 2 * queue_size + 2 slots, 6 at the default. A session that
  negotiated no QoS has ONE lane, so 16 covers it with room to spare; a QoS
  session's lanes share the 16, which covers up to two lanes held at their
  bound at once and any number that are not. A dry pool is back-pressure, not
  an error and not an allocation: a sender asking for room is told the lane is
  congested, as for a full lane, and a frame that must not wait for room (a
  keep-alive, a close) waits for the writer to free a slot, as upstream's
  transport messages wait for a batch.

  At 16 x 65600 a link's pool is about 1 MiB of address space, allocated zeroed
  once when the link is wired, and resident only where frames were written.
  Upstream's QoS link holds 16 batches of 65535 bytes for the same purpose.

  ## section / alignment / cache-policy

  `heap` / 64 / `none`, as `session_rx_pool_ap.scxml`: an AP host has no
  `sram1`, and its bus master is the kernel's socket copy, which needs no cache
  maintenance (ARCHITECTURE section 9.5, the epoll row).
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       xmlns:sce="http://sce.dev/ext"
       sce:kind="buffer-pool" name="session_tx_pool_ap" version="1.0">
  <sce:slot-count>16</sce:slot-count>
  <sce:slot-size>65600</sce:slot-size>
  <sce:section>heap</sce:section>
  <sce:alignment>64</sce:alignment>
  <sce:cache-policy>none</sce:cache-policy>
</scxml>
